                <h2>Why Recharged Amount Often Doesn't Match Account Balance</h2>
<p>This is the most common issue in financial reconciliation. The discrepancy between recharged and actual balance is usually not a platform error but a timing and exchange rate problem.</p>
<p>Fund transfers take time to process, especially during off-hours or across time zones. Additionally, platform-displayed amounts may be in USD, while actual account balance is affected by real-time exchange rates, causing minor differences after conversion.</p>
<p>Recommendation: Use the "actual credited amount" from the platform backend as the standard for recharge records, not bank transfer records. Screenshot and save each recharge, then verify if the timing matches during monthly reconciliation.</p>
<h2>How to Create a Unified Ledger for Multi-Platform, Multi-Account Setups</h2>
<p>Overseas teams typically run ads on Meta, TikTok, Google, and other platforms simultaneously, each with multiple ad accounts. Reconciling separately is time-consuming and error-prone.</p>
<p>The key is establishing an "account numbering system." Assign unique codes to each account across platforms, recording recharge date, amount, currency, and exchange rate. For example, "Meta-US-CAC-001" represents Meta US Customer Acquisition Account 001. Monthly summaries by account code, with all platform spend converted to a single currency, generate one comprehensive report.</p>
<p>Tools like Excel or financial software can build multi-dimensional tables. The critical point is consistent fields: date, account code, platform, recharge amount, spend amount, balance, currency, and exchange rate.</p>
<h2>How to Handle Inconsistent Invoice Headers and Tax Rates</h2>
<p>Different ad platforms have vastly different invoice formats. Meta issues Pro Forma Invoices, Google may provide formal tax invoices, and TikTok's invoicing cycles are irregular. Varying invoice types and tax rates complicate financial archiving.</p>
<p>Recommended approach: Archive using a three-level directory: "Invoice Type + Platform + Month." Upon receiving invoices, first verify that invoice amounts match recharge records, then check if tax information is complete. If tax rate differences affect cost calculations, list them separately as "Tax Rate Adjustment" in financial statements.</p>
<p>For teams with ERP systems, manually enter or sync invoice information via API to avoid losing invoices relying solely on email.</p>
<h2>Where to Start When Finding Discrepancies During Monthly Reconciliation</h2>
<p>When spend doesn't match recharges, investigate in this order:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Timing confirmation</strong>: Is platform spend data delayed? Meta typically has a 1-2 hour reporting lag.</li>
<li><strong>Currency conversion verification</strong>: Check if the recorded exchange rate matches the platform's actual charge rate.</li>
<li><strong>Refund/promotion review</strong>: Confirm if cashbacks or promotions have been deducted from spend.</li>
<li><strong>Account transfer records</strong>: If funds transferred between accounts A and B, verify transfer records are complete.</li>
</ol>
<p>If discrepancies persist after investigation, contact platform support for official spend reports as the final reference.</p>
<h2>Why Recharge Exchange Rates Differ from Actual Charges</h2>
<p>Platform recharge rates are "reference rates," while actual spending may use a different rate structure. Especially for cross-month consumption, exchange rate fluctuations cause differences between recharged and spent amounts after conversion.</p>
<p>This is a universal industry phenomenon, not an error. Handle it by recording exchange rate differences separately under "Foreign Exchange Gains/Losses" rather than advertising spend during monthly reconciliation. Keeping financial and operational accounts separate prevents confusion.</p>
<h2>How to Establish a Standardized Reconciliation SOP</h2>
<p>A replicable reconciliation process should include these steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Daily records</strong>: Screenshot immediately after each recharge, recording amount, time, and account code.</li>
<li><strong>Weekly checks</strong>: Verify each account balance weekly, investigating abnormal spending.</li>
<li><strong>Monthly settlement</strong>: Summarize all account recharge, spend, and balance data monthly, cross-checking with invoices.</li>
<li><strong>Discrepancy reports</strong>: Document discrepancy reasons with platform screenshots as supporting evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Archive storage</strong>: Archive invoices, recharge records, and spend reports together, retaining for at least 12 months.</li>
</ol>
<p>Standardized SOPs reduce reconciliation errors and enable quick handovers when team members change.</p>
<hr />
<p>Overseas advertising financial reconciliation isn't just "getting it done"—it requires systematic management. Recharge, consumption, and invoicing are interconnected; any gap causes mismatches between records and reality. If your team's reconciliation is inefficient or error-prone, prioritize establishing an account coding system and archiving process first. This forms the foundation for unified reconciliation.</p>
